          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "Biological function in living organisms is best understood through an etiological lens that traces back to the selective history of traits rather than through a purely causal-role analysis that focuses solely on current contributions to system capacities. Selected-effect accounts identify a trait's function with the effect for which its ancestors were selected, and this framework explains the normativity of function, including the possibility of malfunction, which a purely dispositional analysis cannot accommodate because it merely describes what a trait is capable of doing without reference to any standard of proper performance that could be violated. \n\nCausal-role accounts following Cummins face difficulties in distinguishing a trait's function from its accidental effects, as they must ultimately relativize function to the explanatory interests of the investigator, thereby surrendering the objectivity of functional attribution and making functions dependent on subjective choices rather than inherent features of biological systems. \n\nEtiological accounts alone vindicate the explanatory force of functional claims, since citing a trait's selected effect answers the historical question of why the trait is present rather than merely describing what it currently does, thereby grounding functional explanations in evolutionary history in a manner that causal-role accounts inherently lack.",
